WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.365
Choosing the base model.

2
00:00:01.645 --> 00:00:03.885
The lab uses Qwen/Qwen2.

3
00:00:04.165 --> 00:00:05.467
5-0.

4
00:00:05.747 --> 00:00:07.027
5B-Instruct.

5
00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:10.699
On the review date its model card listed the Apache 2.

6
00:00:10.979 --> 00:00:17.315
0 license (permissive, commercial use allowed, attribution required) and the model was not gated.

7
00:00:17.595 --> 00:00:28.069
It has about 494 million parameters, so its 16-bit weights are about 1 GB and a LoRA run fits easily on a free Colab GPU or a Mac.

8
00:00:28.349 --> 00:00:34.259
TRL’s own documentation uses it in examples, so the template handling is well trodden.

9
00:00:34.539 --> 00:00:49.685
For your project you’ll choose with more care (week 5 walks through it), but the same three checks apply: a license that permits your use and any adapter you publish, an instruct variant with a chat template, and a size that fits both training and serving.

10
00:00:49.965 --> 00:01:01.229
Start with an instruct model rather than a base model for a narrow task: the instruct model already knows the turn structure and when to stop, and your adapter only has to teach the task.

11
00:01:01.509 --> 00:01:12.368
TRL’s docs warn that when you start from a base model whose tokenizer has a chat template, you may need to align the end-of-sequence token with the template so generations terminate.

12
00:01:12.648 --> 00:01:17.469
Then the honest question: would a well-prompted frontier model do this task well enough?

13
00:01:17.749 --> 00:01:26.475
Fine-tuning pays off when you need low cost per call, low latency, local or private deployment, or a behavior prompting can’t pin down.

14
00:01:26.755 --> 00:01:29.571
Week 2 made that decision; week 4 measures it.
